                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_09\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit changes TransitionHandler.java so that mouse-transparency is set on the whole\ncontainer `view` (view.setMouseTransparent(true)/(false)) instead of on `previousNode`, and it\nremoves the `MOUSE_TRANSPARENT` properties-map hack used to save/restore the previous node's\noriginal mouse-transparent state:\n\n- previousNode.getProperties().put(MOUSE_TRANSPARENT, previousNode.isMouseTransparent());\n  previousNode.setMouseTransparent(true);\n  -> replaced with: view.setMouseTransparent(true);\n\n- previousNode.setMouseTransparent((Boolean) previousNode.getProperties().get(MOUSE_TRANSPARENT));\n  -> replaced with: view.setMouseTransparent(false);\n\nThis exact buggy pattern (marking only `previousNode` transparent via the MOUSE_TRANSPARENT\nproperty, instead of the whole view) was first introduced in candidate_09. That diff adds:\n  previousNode.getProperties().put(MOUSE_TRANSPARENT, previousNode.isMouseTransparent());\n  previousNode.setMouseTransparent(true);\nand\n  previousNode.setMouseTransparent((Boolean) previousNode.getProperties().get(MOUSE_TRANSPARENT));\nalong with the `MOUSE_TRANSPARENT` constant declaration \u2014 i.e., it's the origin of the code the\nfix later removes.\n\ncandidate_10 is a subsequent refactor (wrapping the animation setup in Platform.runLater and\nintroducing an `init` step for AnimationProducer) but it carries the same buggy\npreviousNode-based mouse-transparency logic forward unchanged, so it does not introduce the bug,\nit only inherits it. candidate_11 is identical to the fix diff itself (the bug-fixing change),\nnot a bug-introducing candidate. No candidate before candidate_09 contains any reference to\nMOUSE_TRANSPARENT or previousNode.setMouseTransparent, confirming candidate_09 is the first\ncommit responsible for the incorrect behavior (only the previous view node being restored/marked\nmouse-transparent instead of the whole transition container), which caused the reported bug\nwhere the underlying view's mouse events weren't properly blocked during/after transitions.\n"
                }
